    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "where $\\ell(\\hat{\\Theta}|\\mathbf{z})$ is the likelihood evaluated at the MLE and $\\ell(\\hat{\\Theta}_{r}|\\mathbf{z})$ is the maximum of the likelihood function, subject to the restriction that $r$ parameters unconstrained in the full likelihood analysis are assigned fixed values. For sufficiently large sample size, the LR test statistic is $\\chi_{r}^{2}$-distributed, a $\\chi^{2}$ with $r$ degrees of freedom (Wald 1943).",
